//? Title: CORDIC: Rotations, Gain and Floating Precision
//? Subtitle: Actual circular-mode states, the original four angles and the limits of rounded-period reduction
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an angle in radians, approximate its sine and cosine together using a
sequence of rotations with binary scales. The recurrence is simple, but its
vector length changes, floating arithmetic limits useful precision, and reducing
a large angle by a rounded period can lose its phase. This report derives the
circular CORDIC updates and compensation, follows actual native before/after
states, reproduces the original four-angle comparison and twelve-step path, and
measures errors as the stage count grows. The canonical C++ implementation now
shares one producing kernel with its trace and uses an immutable prepared table.
It is a floating model whose operations and numerical limits are explicit.
)

\toc

> The method: a rotation multiplied by a known gain

A true rotation by dα, for d in {−1,+1}, maps (x,y) to
(x cos α−d y sin α, y cos α+d x sin α). Choose αᵢ=atan(2⁻ⁱ) and drop the
common cos αᵢ factor. With sᵢ=2⁻ⁱ the resulting pseudo-rotation is

\[
x_{i+1}=x_i-d_i s_i y_i,\qquad
y_{i+1}=y_i+d_i s_i x_i,\qquad
z_{i+1}=z_i-d_i\alpha_i.
\]
\alttext(Each step updates both coordinates from the previous pair and subtracts a signed fixed angle from the residual.)

Start at x₀=1, y₀=0 and the reduced target z₀=φ. Choose dᵢ=+1 when zᵢ≥0,
including zero, and −1 otherwise. The two coordinate updates are simultaneous:
y's update uses the old x. The sign corrects an angular overshoot without
restarting. This API executes the requested count; reaching a zero residual does
not stop the next fixed rotation.

The omitted cosine is not free. Squaring and adding the two coordinate updates
gives xᵢ₊₁²+yᵢ₊₁²=(1+sᵢ²)(xᵢ²+yᵢ²). Thus the raw length is amplified by

\[
A_N=\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}\sqrt{1+2^{-2i}},\qquad
K_N=A_N^{-1}=\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}(1+2^{-2i})^{-1/2}.
\]
\alttext(A is the amplification larger than one; K is its reciprocal compensation smaller than one.)

In ideal arithmetic A∞≈1.646760258 and K∞≈0.607252935. The final compensated
coordinates are cos≈K_N x_N and sin≈K_N y_N, with the range-reduction sign
restored afterwards. Use the finite product for the chosen N. The old report
called the reciprocal the gain; here A denotes amplification and K denotes
compensation consistently.

> First worked updates at one radian

The first direction is positive: (1,0) becomes raw (1,1), whose norm is √2.
Its compensated or normalized direction is (1/√2,1/√2), at 45 degrees. Since
1−π/4 is still positive, the next raw state is (0.5,1.5). The third direction
is negative, giving (0.875,1.375). These exact dyadic coordinates can be checked
without evaluating a trigonometric function.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cordic_gain.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_trace(1,3);
  const auto& first=t.steps[0];
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(150,900);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke circle;circle.color={148,163,184};circle.width=1.2;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=2;blue.arrow=true;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke dark;dark.color={51,65,85};dark.width=1.8;dark.arrow=true;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke gold;gold.color={202,138,4};gold.width=1.5;
  d.ellipse({0,0},1,1,{255,255,255,0},circle)
   .path({{0,0},{first.x_before,first.y_before}},dark)
   .path({{0,0},{first.x,first.y}},blue)
   .ellipse({first.x,first.y},.04,.04,{37,99,235},circle)
   .ellipse({first.unit_x,first.unit_y},.04,.04,{202,138,4},gold)
   .path({{first.unit_x,first.unit_y},{.05,1.35}},circle)
   .path({{first.x,first.y},{1.35,1.35}},circle)
   .text({-.7,1.65},"normalised direction\non the unit circle",17)
   .text({1.65,1.58},"raw state (1, 1)",18)
   .text({1.75,-.35},"start (1, 0)",18)
   .text({-.3,-.2},"origin",16)
   .text({.3,2.4},"The first update changes direction and length",22)
   .text({.3,-1.55},std::format("raw norm {:.6f}; compensation K1 = {:.6f}",std::hypot(first.x,first.y),first.inverse_gain),18)
   .save_svg("build/cordic_gain.svg");
  std::print("| stage i | direction | scale | raw x | raw y | residual z | accumulated K |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",s.constant.index,s.direction,s.constant.shift,s.x,s.y,s.z,s.inverse_gain);
  std::print("\n{} actual rotations, {} constant lookups, {} compensation products and {} trace normalizations.\n",t.analysis.work.rotations,t.analysis.work.constant_lookups,t.analysis.work.inverse_gain_products,t.analysis.work.normalizations);
}
