3 actual rotations, 3 constant lookups, 3 compensation products and 3 trace normalizations.
\image(figures/cordic_gain.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first actual raw state lies outside the unit circle. Its normalized direction and the initial vector are shown at equal coordinate scales; compensation corrects the known length amplification.)
\alttext(A dark vector points from the origin to one comma zero. A longer blue vector points to one comma one. A gold dot on that ray lies on the grey unit circle.)

Binary scales can be implemented as shifts in a chosen fixed-point format. This
C++ model uses floating multiplication, subtraction and addition. Its shared
immutable table is lazily prepared with 1,075 atan/sqrt records, then reused.
Each call still accumulates compensation products and performs two final
multiplications; each recorded normalized direction adds hypot and division.
These are algorithmic evaluation counts, not a count of FPGA gates or CPU
instructions, and the report makes no hardware-throughput comparison.

> Folding a half turn: both output signs change

The implementation first computes r=remainder(θ,2πd), using a double constant
πd. If r>πd/2 set φ=r−πd; if r<−πd/2 set φ=r+πd. Either fold removes a
half turn, so both sine and cosine are negated at output. Otherwise φ=r with
no sign change. Inclusive endpoints ±πd/2 are not folded.

For θ=2.5, φ≈−0.641592654. The folded vector is in the fourth quadrant, and
negating both coordinates places the answer in the second. This is the same
fold used for the original four angles.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cordic_fold.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| theta | principal remainder | folded target | negate both |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(double theta:{.5,1.,2.5,-1.2,-3.14159265358979323846,3.14159265358979323846}) {
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::cordic_reduce(theta);
    std::print("| {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {} |\n",theta,r.principal,r.reduced,r.flipped);
  }
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_analyze(2.5,44);
  const double sign=a.reduction.flipped?-1:1;
  const dm::graphics::DiagramPoint restored{a.result.cos,a.result.sin};
  const dm::graphics::DiagramPoint folded{sign*a.result.cos,sign*a.result.sin};
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(190,900);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke edge;edge.color={148,163,184};edge.width=1.2;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke arrow;arrow.color={100,116,139};arrow.width=1.5;arrow.arrow=true;
  d.ellipse({0,0},1,1,{255,255,255,0},edge)
   .path({folded,restored},arrow)
   .ellipse(folded,.035,.035,{37,99,235},edge)
   .ellipse(restored,.035,.035,{220,38,38},edge)
   .path({folded,{1.25,-1.1}},edge).path({restored,{-1.25,1.1}},edge)
   .text({1.25,-1.4},"folded direction",18)
   .text({-1.25,1.4},"restored theta = 2.5",18)
   .text({0,2.2},"Half-turn restoration negates both coordinates",22)
   .text({0,-2.1},"Blue: (cos phi, sin phi); red: (-cos phi, -sin phi)",18)
   .save_svg("build/cordic_fold.svg");
}
