\caption(The folded approximation and the restored approximation are opposite vectors. The cached rotation kernel works only on the folded target; the final sign correction handles the removed half turn.)
\alttext(A blue point in the fourth quadrant and a red point in the second quadrant lie opposite one another on a unit circle, joined by an arrow.)

This is reduction modulo a rounded period, not a high-precision reduction modulo
mathematical 2π. An error in the period is multiplied by the number of removed
turns. Very large finite angles can therefore have large phase error before any
CORDIC stage executes. The final numerical controls below expose that limitation.

> Original comparison and the actual twelve-step path

The four original inputs are 0.5, 1, 2.5 and −1.2 radians, each with 44 stages.
The native trace supplies the original twelve normalized states for θ=1 directly.
The initial state is also drawn. Every normalized point is already on the unit
circle; the connected states zig-zag in angle. The raw radius approaches A_N,
while the accumulated compensation corrects that length.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cordic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| original theta | native sine | native cosine | abs sine error to std | abs cosine error to std |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(double theta:{.5,1.,2.5,-1.2}) {
    const auto s=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos(theta,44);
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n",theta,s.sin,s.cos,std::abs(s.sin-std::sin(theta)),std::abs(s.cos-std::cos(theta)));
  }
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_trace(1,12);
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> path{{t.steps.front().x_before,t.steps.front().y_before}};
  std::print("\n| stage i | direction | residual | raw norm | K prefix | normalised x | normalised y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) {
    path.push_back({s.unit_x,s.unit_y});
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",s.constant.index,s.direction,s.z,std::hypot(s.x,s.y),s.inverse_gain,s.unit_x,s.unit_y);
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(220,900);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke circle;circle.color={148,163,184};circle.width=1.2;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=1.5;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke target;target.color={202,138,4};target.width=2;
  d.ellipse({0,0},1,1,{255,255,255,0},circle).path(path,blue);
  for(const auto& p:path) d.ellipse(p,.014,.014,{37,99,235},blue);
  d.ellipse({std::cos(1.),std::sin(1.)},.045,.045,{255,255,255,0},target)
   .text({0,1.6},"One radian: twelve actual normalized updates",21)
   .text({0,-1.5},"Blue: recorded directions; gold ring: std target",18)
   .save_svg("build/cordic.svg");
  std::print("\nFinal 12-stage pair: sine {:.12f}, cosine {:.12f}; gain compensation {:.12f}.\n",t.analysis.result.sin,t.analysis.result.cos,t.analysis.inverse_gain);
}
