\image(figures/cordic_error.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A fixed 2,001-angle grid shows falling error followed by a floating floor. The ideal bound is for truncation at the reduced target; the measured curve compares the full floating result to std::sin/cos of the original input.)
\alttext(A blue maximum-error curve initially falls with the red ideal finite-stage line and then flattens near binary64 precision, while the red ideal line continues downwards.)

Each successful call performs one range reduction, N table lookups and N raw
updates/compensation products, followed by two final compensation products.
Analysis uses O(1) call scratch after the one-time shared preparation; a trace
owns N constant-size records, O(N) extra storage. The optional complete trace cap
counts all requested records before allocation. The sweep checks its complete
angle-count product before running and uses bounded stage counts, retaining first
worst-angle ties in input order. These counts describe the model, not a runtime
comparison with a tuned libm or a fixed-point hardware implementation.

> Large angles, zero stages and checked contracts

The rounded-period policy is retained explicitly. More rotations can reduce the
kernel residual while leaving a large input phase error unchanged. The following
controls use 64 stages, enough to expose that reduction error separately from the
finite-stage error in the moderate examples.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| original angle | folded target | sine error to std | cosine error to std | norm defect |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(double theta:{1.,1e6,1e12,1e16,1e100,std::numeric_limits<double>::max()}) {
    const auto a=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_analyze(theta,64);
    std::print("| {:.3e} | {:.9f} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n",theta,a.reduction.reduced,std::abs(a.result.sin-std::sin(theta)),std::abs(a.result.cos-std::cos(theta)),std::abs(std::hypot(a.result.sin,a.result.cos)-1));
  }
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_trace(1,0);
  std::print("\nZero stages at theta=1: sine {}, cosine {}, residual {}; no recorded rotations.\n",zero.analysis.result.sin,zero.analysis.result.cos,zero.analysis.residual);
  const auto max=dm::algorithms::cordic_stage_constants(dm::algorithms::cordic_rotation_limit);
  std::print("Stage limit {}; last scale is denorm_min: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::cordic_rotation_limit,max.back().shift==std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min());
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos_trace(1,12,11);return 1; }
  catch(const std::length_error&) {std::print("One-below complete trace cap rejects twelve records.\n");}
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos(std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity(),0);return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {std::print("Nonfinite angles are rejected even for zero stages.\n");}
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cordic_sincos(0,1076);return 1; }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {std::print("Stage 1076 is rejected beyond the nonzero-scale domain.\n");}
  const auto empty=dm::algorithms::cordic_error_sweep({},{0,44});
  std::print("Empty sweeps retain explicit absent extrema: {}.\n",!empty[0].has_samples && !empty[1].has_samples);
}
```
