// result_end

The supported count is 0..1075: stages i=0..1074 cover every nonzero binary64
scale 2⁻ⁱ. Beyond that the scale is zero. Counts outside the domain and nonfinite
angles are rejected before reduction/table access; zero stages remains an explicit
unrotated approximation. The original SinCos two-double field layout and default
count forty remain. There is no early convergence flag, automatic identity fix,
signed-zero sine promise or global guarantee of increasing accuracy with N.

The native implementation assumes the ordinary floating environment. Constants
are prepared once and then read only; returned constants and traces own copies.
Separate calls use independent work state. Analysis and result-only calls retain
no step vectors. Large-angle accuracy to mathematical sin(θ),cos(θ) would require
a more accurate reduction policy, which this API does not claim to supply.

Independent checks compare the rounded-period reduction and the kernel with a
standard-library Decimal oracle at 430-digit working precision. Machin's formula
supplies π and Taylor series supply reference sine/cosine for both the exact
represented input and the actual folded target. This distinguishes a faithful
CORDIC approximation of the folded target from an accurate approximation of a
huge original angle. The optional CTest oracle requires Python's standard library
and no numerical package or language binding.

> Conclusion

The gain derivation, hand-checked updates and native trace explain the actual
circular recurrence. The original four angles and twelve normalized states are
preserved, while tables show stage error, compensation and a floating precision
floor. The large-angle controls demonstrate that a unit-length answer and a tiny
kernel residual can still point in the wrong direction for the original input.
Prepared constants, checked budgets and one shared producing kernel make this
floating model inspectable with explicit numerical scope.

> Reference

- J. E. Volder (1959), *The CORDIC Trigonometric Computing Technique*, IRE Transactions on Electronic Computers EC-8(3), 330–334. [Original paper](https://www.ece.ucdavis.edu/~vojin/CLASSES/EPFL/Papers/3-Volder_CORDIC.pdf), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1109/TEC.1959.5222693). The current implementation demonstrates circular rotation in floating arithmetic; prepared runtime constants, execution histories and error diagnostics are documented additions.
